WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.300
So if you woke up tomorrow and suddenly found out you went completely viral overnight, I mean, how many hours do you think it would take to totally ruin your life?

00:10.600 --> 00:14.260
Oh, wow. I mean, most people would probably say zero hours, right?

00:14.300 --> 00:14.560
Right.

00:14.660 --> 00:17.300
Because society basically tells you it's a golden ticket.

00:17.300 --> 00:28.780
Right. Exactly. Whether you are an entrepreneur trying to build a brand or a creative sharing your work online or, you know, just a really curious person navigating the Internet.

00:28.780 --> 00:36.940
That society has drilled this one single directive into your head from day one, which is just go viral.

00:37.140 --> 00:44.420
Yeah. We're essentially told that getting more eyes on our work is just like the ultimate goal, the guaranteed path to success.

00:44.420 --> 00:52.720
But today we're actually looking at a mathematical proof that shows going viral is not a linear path to success at all.

00:52.720 --> 01:05.620
It's it's actually a highly volatile thermodynamic phase transition and quite possibly a structurally guaranteed path to your absolute disruption, which is quite the hook.

01:05.680 --> 01:08.420
I know. Right. Welcome to the deep dive.

01:08.420 --> 01:14.440
It really is the defining paradigm of contemporary digital culture, isn't it?

01:14.500 --> 01:14.660
Right.

01:14.880 --> 01:24.820
We treat attention as this intrinsic good. We assume that if something is valuable, well, it should just be seen by as many people as humanly possible.

01:24.900 --> 01:27.920
Yeah. Like the Internet is completely built on that premise.

01:27.920 --> 01:33.660
Exactly. It treats attention as a purely neutral resource like sunlight or water.

01:33.660 --> 01:36.300
Yeah. You know, just absorb as much as you can and you'll grow.

01:36.720 --> 01:46.160
OK, let's unpack this, because our mission for this deep dive today is to completely dismantle that modern cult of, you know, building an audience.

01:46.380 --> 01:48.960
I love that phrasing, the cult of building an audience.

01:49.100 --> 01:52.700
It really is one. And our source for today is dense.

01:53.300 --> 01:57.920
It is mind bending. And it honestly completely shifted how I view the Internet.

01:57.920 --> 02:11.840
It's a March 2026 paper by a researcher named Fliction, and it's titled Against Indiscriminate Visibility on the Structural Risks of Virality, Publicity and Exposure.

02:12.060 --> 02:20.340
And what's so fascinating here is how Fliction challenges that fundamental illusion we just talked about, the illusion of neutral attention.

02:20.560 --> 02:22.940
Right. Because attention isn't neutral, is it?

02:22.940 --> 02:30.400
Not at all. The paper rigorously proves that the digital attention economy is a highly unstable environment.

02:30.400 --> 02:38.500
When you increase your visibility, you aren't just like casually expanding your opportunities or your customer base.

02:38.740 --> 02:44.440
You are drastically expanding what the author calls your adversarial surface area.

02:44.800 --> 02:51.360
Your adversarial surface area. I mean, just that phrase alone gives me a sense of impending doom.

02:51.360 --> 02:52.940
It sounds militaristic, right?

02:52.980 --> 02:58.680
It really does. It makes you sound like a military target, not a content creator or a small business owner.

02:59.320 --> 03:04.220
But I want to start by challenging that core assumption of the Internet.

03:04.800 --> 03:08.820
The idea that getting more eyes on your work is always a net positive.

03:09.580 --> 03:15.900
Why is it that simply scaling up an audience actually degrades the quality of the feedback you get?

03:15.900 --> 03:22.500
Like, why can't we just have a million people giving us the exact same polite applause that 10 people would?

03:22.580 --> 03:24.500
Right. The polite applause at scale.

03:24.720 --> 03:25.040
Right.

03:25.420 --> 03:33.920
To understand the mechanics of why that degradation happens, we have to look at the structural asymmetry between rumor and truth.

03:34.000 --> 03:36.660
Okay. Structural asymmetry. Walk me through that.

03:36.660 --> 03:45.060
So, in the physical world, or even in small, tight-knit digital communities, truth actually has a fighting chance.

03:45.480 --> 03:48.000
And that's because verification is possible.

03:48.260 --> 03:50.300
Because you know the people involved.

03:50.400 --> 03:54.000
Exactly. You know them. Or you can go physically look at the thing being discussed.

03:54.000 --> 04:06.740
But at scale, when you're dealing with millions of nodes, the propagation of rumor is mathematically much greater and faster than the propagation of truth.

04:06.880 --> 04:08.960
Because truth is inherently slow.

04:09.160 --> 04:10.740
Very slow. It is constrained.

04:10.980 --> 04:11.180
Yeah.

04:11.340 --> 04:15.120
It requires evidence, requires context, and time to verify.

04:15.500 --> 04:19.360
It's like truth has a speed limit enforced by reality.

04:19.360 --> 04:23.420
Like, you have to actually check sources. You have to read the full context.

04:23.920 --> 04:27.900
While a rumor is basically, I don't know, a sports car with no brakes.

04:28.080 --> 04:29.380
That is a perfect way to put it.

04:30.120 --> 04:33.360
Fast, cheap to produce, and it transmits effortlessly.

04:34.100 --> 04:34.280
Yeah.

04:34.580 --> 04:38.600
Because rumors are usually built on a highly emotional heuristic.

04:39.060 --> 04:41.880
It doesn't ask you to think. It just asks you to react.

04:42.100 --> 04:44.420
And that emotional hook is the accelerant.

04:44.960 --> 04:45.400
Exactly.

04:45.400 --> 04:52.400
And this creates a severe capacity bottleneck for anyone who achieves high visibility.

04:53.300 --> 04:58.160
The correction of a falsehood does not scale proportionally with the falsehood itself.

04:58.500 --> 05:01.320
Wait, say that again? The correction doesn't scale.

05:01.560 --> 05:11.280
Right. So if a single, unverified, damaging claim about you goes viral, it requires a massive, exhausting amount of effort to refute it.

05:11.280 --> 05:16.880
Oh, I see. You have to pull the records, write the statement, provide the context, do a whole video essay.

05:17.000 --> 05:18.960
Yes. All that external work.

05:18.960 --> 05:28.540
And even if you meticulously dismantle the lie, that refutation will literally never reach all the original recipients of the false claim.

05:28.740 --> 05:33.540
Because the architecture of the network just doesn't support equal distribution of corrections.

05:33.540 --> 05:37.220
The algorithm rewards engagement, not accuracy.

05:37.460 --> 05:48.700
Exactly. The conditions that make the rumor spread, like the drama, the simple good versus evil narrative, the outrage, those are the exact conditions that make it almost impossible to dislodge.

05:49.040 --> 05:54.000
Because truth is complicated and, let's be honest, often kind of boring.

05:54.000 --> 06:06.660
Right. So when your visibility increases, your reputational attack surface grows exponentially faster than your physical or mental ability to defend it.

06:06.700 --> 06:10.920
Okay, but this brings up a critical distinction in the paper that I found super interesting.

06:10.920 --> 06:22.380
When we think of reputational attacks, we usually picture, like, coordinated trolls, bot networks, or malicious saboteurs.

06:22.540 --> 06:23.760
The cartoon villains.

06:23.760 --> 06:27.220
Exactly. We picture villains plotting our downfall.

06:27.820 --> 06:34.060
But fliction emphasizes that this degradation doesn't require bad actors at all.

06:34.300 --> 06:39.780
No, not at all. It is driven entirely by ordinary, everyday human motives.

06:39.940 --> 06:42.940
Just scaled up to unnatural proportions.

06:43.140 --> 06:47.180
And this is the part that honestly forced me to re-evaluate my own behavior online.

06:47.380 --> 06:52.500
Just normal people doing normal things, but the sheer scale of it turns it toxic.

06:52.500 --> 07:00.340
Yeah. Think about community cohesion or self-protection or honestly just the casual transmission of a juicy piece of gossip.

07:00.720 --> 07:00.840
Right.

07:01.040 --> 07:09.040
When a community is organized around a specific belief system or a specific market and you introduce a novel or disruptive idea to them,

07:09.040 --> 07:12.180
they rarely see it as an intellectual invitation.

07:12.360 --> 07:13.720
They perceive it as a threat.

07:13.720 --> 07:16.200
An existential provocation, yes.

07:16.760 --> 07:19.080
Their reaction is inherently defensive.

07:19.740 --> 07:23.400
The paper uses a really grounded example here about a restaurateur.

07:23.400 --> 07:32.820
Let's say someone opens a new restaurant in a neighborhood and they manage to undercut local prices, offering a cheaper, more accessible product.

07:33.080 --> 07:34.540
A classic market disruption.

07:34.800 --> 07:35.260
Exactly.

07:35.260 --> 07:40.960
The incumbent business owners on that street experienced that as a tangible loss.

07:41.620 --> 07:48.360
The rational response for them isn't to walk over and congratulate the new chef on their supply chain efficiency.

07:48.660 --> 07:48.980
Right.

07:49.120 --> 07:52.140
They aren't going to throw a party for the guy stealing their customers.

07:52.280 --> 07:52.680
No.

07:53.540 --> 07:54.400
It's to resist.

07:54.560 --> 07:58.500
So they might start a little rumor about the quality of the new guy's ingredients.

07:59.040 --> 08:01.860
Like, oh, he's definitely using frozen fish.

08:01.940 --> 08:03.060
That's why it's so cheap.

08:03.060 --> 08:08.200
And importantly, they aren't evil supervillains quarreling their mustaches when they do this.

08:08.280 --> 08:08.560
Right.

08:08.680 --> 08:12.300
They are reacting to the topology of the evaluation system.

08:12.300 --> 08:21.200
In a system of relative evaluation, like competing for a limited customer base or even grading on a curve in the classroom,

08:22.020 --> 08:26.340
one person's success inherently reduces another person's standing.

08:26.340 --> 08:33.300
Which means resentment in these systems is not a moral failure of the individuals involved.

08:34.100 --> 08:37.180
It is a structural consequence of the environment.

08:37.540 --> 08:41.220
So the conflict is baked into the geometry of the market itself.

08:42.440 --> 08:49.600
Any increase in your visibility within a competitive landscape naturally induces adversarial flows.

08:49.800 --> 08:50.700
That's the core of it.

08:50.780 --> 08:51.100
Yes.

08:51.100 --> 08:54.800
But, okay, I'm trying to find a loophole here, though.

08:55.340 --> 09:00.120
I hear what you're saying, but isn't pushback just part of putting yourself out there?

09:00.320 --> 09:00.820
How do you mean?

09:01.140 --> 09:07.180
Like, if you want to be a thought leader or sell a product, shouldn't you just accept that some people won't like it?

09:07.540 --> 09:09.020
Let me try an analogy here.

09:09.420 --> 09:12.640
It's like opening a small artisanal bakery.

09:13.160 --> 09:13.600
Okay.

09:13.700 --> 09:14.520
I like bakeries.

09:14.520 --> 09:14.840
Right.

09:15.440 --> 09:20.580
So if you serve 10 locals who live on your street, you get nuanced, helpful feedback.

09:20.820 --> 09:24.360
They might say, hey, a little more salt in the sourdough tomorrow, please.

09:24.580 --> 09:24.660
Sure.

09:25.500 --> 09:26.640
Manageable feedback.

09:27.180 --> 09:27.700
Exactly.

09:27.700 --> 09:46.860
But if a million people suddenly teleport into your bakery overnight, even if 99% of them are perfectly neutral or actively love the bread, the 1% who arbitrarily hate the smell of yeast or who are allergic to gluten and furious that you don't caver to them are going to try to burn the place down.

09:47.000 --> 09:47.300
Yes.

09:47.740 --> 09:49.080
And the problem is...

09:49.080 --> 09:53.160
You physically cannot bake bread and fight fires at the same time.

09:53.340 --> 09:55.520
That captures the dynamic perfectly.

09:55.520 --> 09:59.080
The issue isn't that pushback exists.

09:59.860 --> 10:05.660
The issue is the ratio of incoming flow to your corrective capacity.

10:05.740 --> 10:06.580
The ratio.

10:07.000 --> 10:11.000
The algorithm can put your bakery in front of a million people instantly.

10:11.280 --> 10:13.460
Your distribution scales infinitely.

10:13.460 --> 10:25.940
But your personal bandwidth to defend your work, to clarify your intentions, and as you said, to sweep up the broken glass, that remains exactly the same as it was when you were serving 10 people.

10:26.020 --> 10:27.420
Because I'm just one person.

10:27.560 --> 10:28.020
Exactly.

10:28.160 --> 10:28.320
Yeah.

10:28.600 --> 10:32.060
You're a biological entity trying to deal with algorithmic flow.

10:32.300 --> 10:32.560
Yeah.

10:32.680 --> 10:33.460
The fundamental mismatch.

10:33.460 --> 10:42.320
And here's where it gets really interesting, because the paper doesn't just leave this as a cool sociological observation or a warning about burnout.

10:42.720 --> 10:44.100
No, it takes it much further.

10:44.100 --> 10:53.320
It asks, if rumor scales faster than truth, and if human capacity is fixed, how do we systematically measure this risk?

10:54.020 --> 10:57.620
And this is where affliction does something entirely unexpected.

10:58.100 --> 11:02.340
They model internet virality using theoretical physics.

11:02.600 --> 11:04.160
The RSVP framework.

11:04.160 --> 11:04.560
Right.

11:04.720 --> 11:06.760
The relativistic scalar vector plenum.

11:06.880 --> 11:09.680
I mean, it sounds incredibly intimidating on the surface.

11:09.920 --> 11:10.700
It sounds terrifying.

11:11.040 --> 11:14.800
But the underlying logic is actually quite elegant.

11:15.180 --> 11:25.460
It takes all these informal observations we just discussed about internet pylons, cancel culture, and virality, and formalizes them into a field theoretic model.

11:25.680 --> 11:32.740
It maps social dynamics onto the physics of fluid dynamics and electromagnetism, which is wild.

11:32.740 --> 11:34.220
It is wild, but it works.

11:34.480 --> 11:42.600
So I want to break down the three main variables of this RSVP framework, and I want to make sure we keep it grounded so we don't lose anyone in the math.

11:42.660 --> 11:43.140
That's good.

11:43.200 --> 11:43.740
Let's do it.

11:43.800 --> 11:43.960
Okay.

11:44.060 --> 11:45.200
First, we have visibility.

11:45.840 --> 11:49.160
The paper uses the Greek letter phi for this.

11:49.660 --> 11:51.100
This is the scalar potential.

11:51.300 --> 11:58.020
In physics, a scalar field just assigns a single value to every point in space, like temperature in a room.

11:58.480 --> 11:58.660
Right.

11:59.020 --> 11:59.440
Exactly.

11:59.440 --> 12:06.000
In this model, phi is simply the amount of exposure you have, how hot your spotlight is.

12:06.180 --> 12:06.440
Okay.

12:06.580 --> 12:06.980
Got it.

12:07.420 --> 12:08.480
Visibility is phi.

12:08.920 --> 12:14.900
Moving to the second variable, we have interpretive flow, which is denoted by a vector V.

12:15.100 --> 12:17.080
A vector, so it has direction.

12:17.420 --> 12:17.700
Right.

12:18.020 --> 12:22.880
If the scalar potential is the temperature in the room, the vector field is the wind.

12:23.260 --> 12:25.580
It has both magnitude and direction.

12:25.580 --> 12:33.260
This represents the actual movement of opinions, attention, opportunism, and hostility across the network.

12:33.420 --> 12:37.560
It's the current of human reaction moving toward or away from your work.

12:37.560 --> 12:38.440
Precisely.

12:38.900 --> 12:42.100
And third, we have reputational entropy, or S.

12:42.300 --> 12:45.100
And entropy in physics is a measure of disorder.

12:45.340 --> 12:45.600
Right.

12:45.720 --> 12:54.020
So here, it is the measurement of distortion, the density of rumors, and the overall narrative chaos surrounding your name or your brand.

12:54.020 --> 13:00.260
Okay, so we have visibility, interpretive flow, and entropy, phi, V, and S.

13:00.260 --> 13:08.240
And when you combine these three variables into a set of differential equations, the math reveals something profound about the architecture of the internet.

13:08.940 --> 13:16.080
As visibility, your phi, increases, it inevitably steepens the gradients of the social field.

13:16.480 --> 13:22.260
Let me translate this math into a visual, because this totally clicked for me when I was reading your notes on this.

13:22.360 --> 13:22.920
Go for it.

13:22.920 --> 13:28.460
Visibility isn't just a spotlight shining on you, passively illuminating your work.

13:28.960 --> 13:30.920
It's a gravitational well.

13:31.200 --> 13:32.480
A gravitational well.

13:32.560 --> 13:37.180
Yes, the heavier you get, the more visible you become, the stronger your gravity.

13:37.820 --> 13:43.320
And the stronger your gravity, the more space debris you aggressively pull into your orbit.

13:43.680 --> 13:45.420
That's a great way to visualize it.

13:45.420 --> 13:52.620
You pull in the interpretive flow, critics, opportunists, people having a bad day who just want to yell at someone on their lunch break.

13:52.760 --> 13:54.020
You pull them all in.

13:54.020 --> 14:01.520
Because you are increasing your coupling to a highly heterogeneous environment.

14:01.940 --> 14:04.100
Heterogeneous meaning mixed.

14:04.360 --> 14:04.540
Right.

14:04.800 --> 14:07.240
Meaning people have vastly different priors.

14:07.400 --> 14:07.560
Yeah.

14:07.820 --> 14:10.660
Different political triggers, different personal agendas.

14:10.660 --> 14:22.160
When your massive gravity pulls in all those different interpretive frameworks at once, that massive influx inevitably and mathematically generates reputational entropy.

14:22.760 --> 14:24.620
So the distortion isn't an accident.

14:24.800 --> 14:25.420
Not at all.

14:25.700 --> 14:25.860
Yeah.

14:25.920 --> 14:32.100
It's a product of the friction between all those conflicting interpretations colliding around your work.

14:32.220 --> 14:32.980
But hold on.

14:33.180 --> 14:34.580
Let me clay devil's advocate here.

14:35.180 --> 14:38.360
What if you're right?

14:38.960 --> 14:40.080
Right about what?

14:40.080 --> 14:41.340
Whatever you post.

14:42.380 --> 14:48.620
What if your viral post is morally pure, factually accurate, and ethically aligned?

14:48.780 --> 14:52.180
What if you're just posting, I don't know, a fundraiser for a children's hospital?

14:53.080 --> 14:57.160
Doesn't the truth value if your post protect you from that space debris?

14:57.660 --> 15:01.200
Because we are raised to believe the truth is a shield.

15:01.480 --> 15:02.500
Yeah, we are.

15:02.860 --> 15:06.660
But this is perhaps the most sobering conclusion in the entire paper.

15:06.660 --> 15:15.820
Fliction explicitly proves that moral alignment or factual correctness does not protect you from reputational entropy.

15:16.040 --> 15:16.680
Wait, really?

15:16.740 --> 15:18.040
It offers zero protection?

15:18.280 --> 15:18.640
None.

15:19.340 --> 15:23.540
The intrinsic truth value of a signal does not stabilize the equation.

15:24.400 --> 15:29.060
Within this mathematical framework, correctness is not a stabilizing term.

15:29.060 --> 15:33.880
Entropy is driven by interaction density, not by truth.

15:33.880 --> 15:34.800
Oh, wow.

15:35.260 --> 15:38.480
I really want you, the listener, to pause and consider that.

15:38.560 --> 15:40.180
It does not matter how right you are.

15:40.240 --> 15:42.220
It doesn't matter how pure your intentions are.

15:42.420 --> 15:49.680
If a million people are interacting with your statement, the sheer density of that interaction guarantees that the meaning will distort.

15:49.680 --> 16:03.000
Like, people will argue about the administrative costs of the children's hospital you're raising money for, or they will accuse you of using it for a tax write-off, or they will claim you're ignoring a different, more important disease.

16:03.000 --> 16:14.420
And the signal degrades faster than it can be transmitted, because rumor, cynicism, and distortion are what physicists would call low-energy excitations.

16:14.500 --> 16:17.920
Low-energy excitations, meaning they're easy to do.

16:18.100 --> 16:20.420
Incredibly cheap and easy to produce.

16:21.100 --> 16:24.100
Someone can tweet a cynical accusation in four seconds.

16:24.100 --> 16:54.020
Okay, so if entropy, this chaotic distortion, is mathematically guaranteed to rise with visibility,

16:54.340 --> 17:00.380
and if being right doesn't save you, is it actually impossible to safely go viral?

17:00.520 --> 17:03.060
Like, is there absolutely no way to hack the system?

17:03.200 --> 17:05.580
The paper proves that it is impossible, period.

17:05.920 --> 17:06.680
There is no hack.

17:06.760 --> 17:06.980
None.

17:07.160 --> 17:07.520
None.

17:08.060 --> 17:10.600
Which leads us to the formal proof in the text.

17:12.000 --> 17:14.500
The no-go theorem for safe virality.

17:14.740 --> 17:15.840
The no-go theorem.

17:16.520 --> 17:18.840
It sounds like a law of quantum mechanics.

17:18.840 --> 17:22.760
Like, you cannot travel faster than light, and you cannot safely go viral.

17:22.760 --> 17:26.360
It functions very much like a law of physics within this framework.

17:27.100 --> 17:40.040
It is a formal mathematical proof demonstrating that unbounded visibility, coupled with bounded human protection, makes safe virality a mathematical impossibility.

17:40.280 --> 17:45.000
And it relies on three very basic, undeniable assumptions.

17:45.540 --> 17:45.740
Yes.

17:45.740 --> 17:47.480
Let's test these assumptions, actually.

17:47.600 --> 17:49.020
I want to see if we can break the theorem.

17:49.200 --> 17:49.980
What's the first one?

17:50.120 --> 17:50.960
Assumption one.

17:51.680 --> 17:53.180
Interaction produces distortion.

17:53.420 --> 17:54.520
We just covered this.

17:54.740 --> 17:59.360
More people talking means more telephone game mutations of your original point.

17:59.420 --> 17:59.660
Right.

17:59.880 --> 18:05.740
Given the heterogeneity of the internet, every interaction carries a non-zero probability of misinterpretation.

18:06.700 --> 18:08.080
Okay, I can't argue with that.

18:08.080 --> 18:12.180
Anyone who has ever read a comment section knows that interaction produces distortion.

18:12.420 --> 18:13.480
So what's assumption two?

18:14.020 --> 18:14.840
Assumption two.

18:16.040 --> 18:18.420
Visibility induces interaction.

18:19.820 --> 18:23.380
You cannot be highly visible without people reacting to you.

18:24.000 --> 18:28.920
By definition, being seen on an algorithmic platform invites engagement.

18:29.320 --> 18:33.480
Right, because the platforms literally design the interface to force engagement.

18:33.480 --> 18:37.300
The reply button is right there, constantly begging you to click it.

18:37.780 --> 18:39.180
Okay, assumption three.

18:39.520 --> 18:40.360
Assumption three.

18:41.260 --> 18:43.920
Human capacity to correct distortion is finite.

18:44.660 --> 18:46.460
You only have 24 hours in a day.

18:47.260 --> 18:55.880
You have a limited amount of emotional bandwidth, cognitive stamina, and literally typing speed to reply to people who are misunderstanding you.

18:55.940 --> 18:56.660
Okay, yes.

18:57.200 --> 18:59.540
All three of those are undeniably true.

18:59.540 --> 19:05.060
So, interaction distorts, visibility forces interaction, and I am a human who needs to sleep.

19:05.600 --> 19:15.080
Therefore, if you accept those three mild assumptions, the math dictates that there is no trajectory where visibility diverges to infinity,

19:15.420 --> 19:20.180
where you go massively viral while maintaining a safe, stable reputation.

19:20.500 --> 19:22.520
Instability is structurally guaranteed.

19:22.520 --> 19:30.400
It's structurally guaranteed, because the incoming wave of distortion will mathematically always eclipse your finite capacity to correct it.

19:30.560 --> 19:32.980
It's like trying to bail out a sinking ship with a teacup.

19:33.360 --> 19:34.000
A teacup.

19:34.160 --> 19:34.720
Exactly.

19:34.880 --> 19:37.220
Your teacup is your corrective capacity.

19:37.640 --> 19:43.540
It's your ability to tweet a clarification or post a follow-up video explaining what you actually meant.

19:43.820 --> 19:49.200
But the ocean pouring into the ship is the Internet's interpretive flow.

19:49.400 --> 19:51.700
And the ocean doesn't care about your teacup.

19:51.700 --> 19:52.300
No.

19:52.660 --> 19:59.320
It doesn't matter how ethically pure your teacup is or how eloquently you scoop the water.

20:00.100 --> 20:02.860
The math of the ocean will drown you.

20:03.300 --> 20:06.380
That analogy perfectly illustrates the asymmetry.

20:07.220 --> 20:10.820
And there's a corollary to this theorem that is equally important.

20:12.320 --> 20:15.620
Risk scales super linearly with exposure.

20:16.080 --> 20:19.780
Super linearly, meaning it doesn't just double when your audience doubles.

20:19.980 --> 20:20.240
Right.

20:20.240 --> 20:26.400
So if I go from 10,000 followers to 20,000 followers, my risk doesn't just go up by a factor of two.

20:26.580 --> 20:26.920
Correct.

20:27.120 --> 20:28.960
It curves upward exponentially.

20:29.840 --> 20:36.720
As your visibility increases, the density of overlapping conflicting networks you're exposed to multiplies.

20:36.840 --> 20:38.660
So the danger accelerates.

20:38.680 --> 20:39.980
It accelerates rapidly.

20:39.980 --> 20:51.080
The inflection point where the risk of distortion and reputational damage actually overtakes the benefit of the exposure happens long before you hit the numbers people typically aim for.

20:51.180 --> 20:51.520
Really?

20:51.520 --> 20:52.400
Oh, yeah.

20:52.400 --> 20:55.660
You don't need a million followers to hit the danger zone.

20:56.180 --> 21:05.780
Depending on your context, it might happen at 10,000 or even 1,000 if the interpretive mismatch between you and that specific audience is high enough.

21:05.780 --> 21:06.300
Wow.

21:06.780 --> 21:07.180
Okay.

21:07.500 --> 21:11.700
I have to stop you here, though, because I know what you, the listener, are thinking.

21:11.940 --> 21:16.080
And I was thinking the exact same thing when I was reading this section of the paper.

21:16.180 --> 21:16.500
Exactly.

21:16.500 --> 21:25.940
We look around and we see massive corporations, A-list celebrities, and huge influencers going viral literally all the time.

21:26.060 --> 21:26.580
Sure we do.

21:26.760 --> 21:32.100
They live in that high visibility, high risk zone permanently, and they seem totally fine.

21:32.840 --> 21:40.120
So if the no-go theorem is an absolute mathematical law, why does Taylor Swift still have a career?

21:40.340 --> 21:41.920
Why them and not us?

21:41.920 --> 21:48.780
And this naturally progresses to the paper's most cutting sociological insight, because you're right to ask that.

21:49.260 --> 21:54.680
The no-go theorem states that safe virality is impossible with bounded human protection.

21:54.920 --> 21:56.060
Ah, bounded.

21:56.340 --> 21:56.540
Right.

21:56.900 --> 22:02.440
It introduces a critical variable called protective capacity, denoted by the letter P.

22:02.960 --> 22:04.120
Protective capacity.

22:04.320 --> 22:05.900
So that's the size of your teacup.

22:06.220 --> 22:09.900
Or maybe more accurately, the armor around your ship.

22:10.140 --> 22:10.740
Precisely.

22:10.740 --> 22:20.400
For a massive corporation or a global celebrity, P is not just their personal ability to tweet a clarification.

22:21.420 --> 22:33.380
Their P represents a massive battery of lawyers, public relations teams, crisis management firms, institutional backing, and immense financial wealth.

22:33.740 --> 22:36.380
So they aren't just one human bailing water.

22:36.380 --> 22:42.300
No, they have an artificially inflated, structurally reinforced capacity to correct narratives.

22:42.940 --> 22:50.360
They can threaten defamation lawsuits, they can buy ad space, and they can absorb reputational damage without losing their livelihood.

22:50.800 --> 22:59.380
While an independent creative or a freelance writer or a local business owner has a P that basically consists of their own mental health and a Wi-Fi connection.

22:59.380 --> 22:59.860
Exactly.

22:59.860 --> 23:00.380
Exactly.

23:00.860 --> 23:03.460
I mean, think about your own P right now listening to this.

23:03.980 --> 23:08.860
If a million people misinterpreted your tweet today, who is fielding your angry emails?

23:09.440 --> 23:09.940
No one.

23:10.080 --> 23:12.540
It's just you staring at your phone in your living room.

23:12.540 --> 23:18.180
And this vast difference in resources gives us the inequality amplification theorem.

23:18.180 --> 23:19.780
Okay, lay that out for me.

23:20.100 --> 23:21.260
Imagine two people.

23:21.780 --> 23:23.640
One is a highly resourced celebrity.

23:24.340 --> 23:27.780
The other is an independent writer with very few resources.

23:27.780 --> 23:37.260
They both post the exact same controversial statement, and they both receive the exact same massive amount of viral visibility.

23:38.060 --> 23:39.060
Identical input.

23:39.340 --> 23:43.200
Okay, so the physics of the environment are exactly the same for both of them.

23:43.340 --> 23:51.200
Yes, but the theorem proves that the low-resource person will cross the threshold into reputational destruction,

23:51.580 --> 23:56.140
while the high-resource person will remain completely operational.

23:56.400 --> 23:57.400
Because of their P.

23:57.400 --> 23:58.160
Because of their P.

23:59.140 --> 24:01.260
The celebrity deploys their PR team.

24:01.680 --> 24:06.300
They issue coordinated focus group statements across multiple friendly media channels.

24:06.620 --> 24:10.720
They absorb the initial shock wave, and they stabilize the narrative.

24:10.880 --> 24:11.980
And the independent writer.

24:12.140 --> 24:15.740
They face the exact same shock wave with only their personal time.

24:16.120 --> 24:18.840
They quickly exhaust their cognitive bandwidth.

24:19.460 --> 24:25.160
The rumor outpaces their ability to correct it, and they are structurally removed from the visible field.

24:25.160 --> 24:26.700
They lose their job.

24:26.700 --> 24:28.200
Their clients drop them.

24:28.300 --> 24:29.840
They literally delete their accounts.

24:30.900 --> 24:37.620
Man, this completely reframes how we view credentials and status, doesn't it?

24:37.620 --> 24:39.900
The paper even talks about this, right?

24:39.900 --> 24:46.480
Like, having a degree from an Ivy League school or a title at a major legacy publication.

24:46.680 --> 24:47.380
It does, yes.

24:47.380 --> 24:58.940
Because we usually think of credentials purely as proof of knowledge or expertise, but Flixian argues they function as something much more defensive in the digital age.

24:59.520 --> 25:01.200
They are stability licenses.

25:01.200 --> 25:04.720
A stability license is a brilliant way to conceptualize it.

25:05.200 --> 25:18.100
A credential signals to the algorithm and the public that you have the institutional backing to safely operate at higher visibility levels without catastrophic exposure to that unstable regime.

25:18.260 --> 25:19.360
So it's an armor buff.

25:20.280 --> 25:20.700
Basically.

25:21.020 --> 25:25.220
If a random person posts a radical economic theory, they are mocked and dismissed.

25:25.220 --> 25:34.700
If a Harvard professor posts the exact same theory, they are debated respectfully, because the Harvard credential proves they have a higher baseline P.

25:35.180 --> 25:37.500
The institution acts as a shock absorber.

25:37.840 --> 25:40.040
So the internet looks like this great democratizer.

25:40.500 --> 25:43.500
The promise of Web 2.0 was that there are no gatekeepers.

25:43.780 --> 25:44.940
Anyone can go viral.

25:45.260 --> 25:46.400
Anyone can be a star.

25:46.660 --> 25:46.880
Right.

25:47.060 --> 25:48.080
The grand promise.

25:48.480 --> 25:51.980
But exposure doesn't bypass inequality.

25:52.720 --> 25:55.080
It dynamically amplifies it.

25:55.220 --> 26:06.740
The physics of virality literally punish the unprotected, which brings us to a massive revelation in the paper that made me want to throw my laptop out the window.

26:06.900 --> 26:08.260
The selection bias theorem.

26:08.440 --> 26:08.900
Yes.

26:09.740 --> 26:13.820
The selection bias theorem for observed visibility success.

26:13.820 --> 26:25.440
This theorem explains why the culture continues to aggressively push the go viral narrative, despite the mathematical guarantees of destruction for the average person.

26:25.600 --> 26:25.840
Right.

26:26.040 --> 26:38.440
Because the gurus on stages telling you to build an audience, the marketing experts telling you to maximize your reach, the influencers selling courses on how to go viral, they are the survivors.

26:38.440 --> 26:39.660
They are the anomaly.

26:39.880 --> 26:40.380
Exactly.

26:40.740 --> 26:40.880
Yeah.

26:40.980 --> 26:51.900
The people who were completely destroyed by virality, whose mental health collapsed, whose small businesses were ruined by a bad faith cancellation campaign over a misunderstood joke.

26:51.900 --> 26:54.300
They aren't giving TED Talks.

26:54.300 --> 26:54.960
No, they aren't.

26:55.000 --> 26:57.940
They aren't writing best-selling books on how to succeed on the internet.

26:58.080 --> 27:01.140
They have been entirely erased from the visible sample.

27:01.140 --> 27:05.220
It's classic survivor bias, formalized into a theorem.

27:05.220 --> 27:14.780
We are only observing the trajectories of agents whose protective capacity was high enough to withstand the entropy.

27:15.080 --> 27:22.920
We look at the winners and assume their strategy, which is maximizing visibility, is what caused their success.

27:23.220 --> 27:23.520
Right.

27:23.740 --> 27:25.860
We confuse the cause and the effect.

27:26.020 --> 27:32.820
We fail to realize that their pre-existing protective capacity is what allowed them to survive the strategy.

27:32.820 --> 27:36.460
I really want you to think about the advice you consume daily.

27:37.120 --> 27:39.780
Think about the business podcasts you listen to.

27:40.240 --> 27:44.640
Are you taking swimming lessons from a fish who doesn't realize you don't have gills?

27:45.200 --> 27:46.360
That's a great analogy.

27:46.520 --> 27:48.220
The fish says, just jump in the ocean.

27:48.320 --> 27:48.700
It's great.

27:48.840 --> 27:49.740
The water is fine.

27:49.820 --> 27:50.620
Just keep swimming.

27:51.160 --> 27:52.880
But you are human.

27:53.160 --> 27:59.160
You don't have the institutional gills to breathe under that kind of algorithmic, hydrostatic pressure.

27:59.160 --> 28:05.180
The fish isn't malicious, it just fundamentally doesn't understand your structural vulnerability.

28:05.860 --> 28:12.040
We drastically underestimate the true risk of exposure because the casualties are invisible to us.

28:12.440 --> 28:14.700
The system only displays the survivors.

28:15.280 --> 28:17.900
So let's explore the worst case scenario.

28:18.840 --> 28:20.360
Let's say someone does go viral.

28:20.580 --> 28:26.300
They don't have the massive protective capacity and they start drowning in that reputational entropy.

28:26.740 --> 28:28.900
The space debris is tearing their ship apart.

28:28.900 --> 28:29.400
Exactly.

28:29.760 --> 28:31.060
Can they just delete the post?

28:31.180 --> 28:36.140
Like, can you just turn off your phone, issue a quick apology, log off, and go back to normal?

28:36.520 --> 28:37.880
The short answer is no.

28:38.060 --> 28:39.180
And the long answer?

28:39.560 --> 28:42.680
The long answer is found in coherence thermodynamics.

28:43.280 --> 28:47.900
Fliction maps a theory by a researcher named Barton onto this visibility framework.

28:48.520 --> 28:53.220
The idea is that high visibility literally causes semantic heating.

28:53.700 --> 28:54.620
Semantic heating.

28:54.960 --> 28:58.400
Okay, we need to unpack this metaphor because it's incredibly vivid.

28:58.400 --> 29:07.980
When you pump massive amounts of attention, which is energy, into a single piece of work or a single person, you are increasing its semantic temperature.

29:09.020 --> 29:14.840
And just like physical matter, social systems go through phase transitions when heated.

29:15.080 --> 29:15.360
Right.

29:15.560 --> 29:18.180
Matter goes from solid to liquid to gas.

29:18.340 --> 29:21.080
And reputations go through a very similar process.

29:21.080 --> 29:22.420
Four phases.

29:22.420 --> 29:28.640
Let's walk through these because it perfectly describes the life cycle of almost every internet creator I've ever followed.

29:29.240 --> 29:32.860
Phase I is the low visibility coherent regime.

29:33.020 --> 29:33.160
Right.

29:33.600 --> 29:36.040
This is your small foundational community.

29:36.580 --> 29:37.400
High context.

29:37.760 --> 29:38.420
High trust.

29:38.680 --> 29:39.180
Low entropy.

29:39.940 --> 29:43.740
You share an idea, a piece of art, or a business plan.

29:43.740 --> 29:47.140
And people interpret it generally as you intended.

29:47.620 --> 29:48.520
Because they know you.

29:48.640 --> 29:49.520
Because they know you.

29:50.060 --> 29:53.960
Signal integrity is high because the semantic temperature is low.

29:54.340 --> 29:55.000
It's stable.

29:55.320 --> 29:56.620
Then you get a bit of traction.

29:57.080 --> 29:58.460
An algorithm picks you up.

29:58.540 --> 30:00.000
Someone big retweets you.

30:00.160 --> 30:01.900
And you move into phase two.

30:02.180 --> 30:04.200
The competitive visibility regime.

30:04.360 --> 30:06.700
Here, visibility is rising significantly.

30:06.700 --> 30:10.420
You're entering broader markets, larger algorithm feeds.

30:11.000 --> 30:16.040
You start seeing relative evaluation where strangers are comparing you to others in your field.

30:16.540 --> 30:18.900
And adversarial flows begin to emerge.

30:19.060 --> 30:20.780
You get your first real haters.

30:21.160 --> 30:21.700
Exactly.

30:22.000 --> 30:23.020
Entropy is increasing.

30:23.180 --> 30:24.160
The temperature is rising.

30:24.660 --> 30:28.980
But it's still somewhat manageable if you have a decent teacup to bail the water.

30:29.160 --> 30:31.420
And then the true virality hits.

30:31.860 --> 30:34.120
The semantic heating boils over.

30:34.800 --> 30:35.980
You hit phase three.

30:35.980 --> 30:38.680
The turbulent virality regime.

30:39.260 --> 30:40.240
Runaway distortion.

30:41.380 --> 30:47.420
Your narrative entirely fragments into a dozen computing, contradictory interpretations.

30:48.120 --> 30:50.880
The system is no longer self-correcting.

30:51.180 --> 30:54.160
It is actively generating chaos.

30:54.320 --> 30:56.620
People aren't even reacting to you anymore, are they?

30:56.700 --> 30:59.680
No, they are reacting to other people's reactions of you.

30:59.920 --> 31:01.860
Your reputation is destabilized.

31:01.960 --> 31:03.560
It's exactly like water.

31:04.260 --> 31:05.460
Phase one is ice.

31:05.460 --> 31:07.740
It's solid, structured, coherent.

31:07.740 --> 31:10.040
Phase two is liquid water.

31:10.280 --> 31:10.860
It flows.

31:11.120 --> 31:14.240
It changes shape based on the container, but it's still manageable.

31:14.760 --> 31:16.180
Phase three is steam.

31:16.620 --> 31:18.620
You have completely lost containment.

31:18.620 --> 31:21.680
The particles are flying everywhere, bouncing off the walls.

31:21.680 --> 31:26.260
And here's the terrifying part about phase three and why you can't just delete the app.

31:26.480 --> 31:29.580
The paper talks about this concept of hysteresis.

31:29.820 --> 31:30.060
Yes.

31:30.280 --> 31:33.640
Hysteresis is a crucial property of systems with memory.

31:34.500 --> 31:37.800
In physics, it means the state of a system depends on its history.

31:37.800 --> 31:42.320
Simply put, the path up is not the same as the path down.

31:42.320 --> 31:46.560
You can't just unboil a pot of water instantly by turning off the stove.

31:46.780 --> 31:48.680
The heat is already in the water.

31:49.140 --> 31:49.580
Exactly.

31:49.980 --> 31:56.220
If you hit phase three, turbulent virality, and you realize it's a nightmare, and you delete

31:56.220 --> 32:02.280
the viral tweet or take down the controversial video, effectively reducing your visibility,

32:02.280 --> 32:07.040
your phi, back to zero, the damage doesn't disappear.

32:07.460 --> 32:07.860
Wow.

32:07.980 --> 32:09.300
The system has a memory kernel.

32:10.080 --> 32:15.820
The reputational entropy you generated leaves a permanent residue because, as we established

32:15.820 --> 32:19.280
earlier, truth is slow and rumor is fast.

32:19.780 --> 32:24.660
Rumors decay like a power law, which means they drop off quickly at first, but then leave

32:24.660 --> 32:27.600
a very long, agonizingly slow tale.

32:27.600 --> 32:33.320
Even if you hide, fragments of that distorted narrative remain cached in search engines,

32:33.520 --> 32:35.340
in forums, in people's memories.

32:35.560 --> 32:39.160
True recovery requires the passage of an immense amount of time.

32:39.640 --> 32:43.240
You don't just magically return to your phase-eye pre-exposure baseline.

32:43.480 --> 32:48.680
The paper uses a brilliantly simple analogy of a ball in a tilting bowl to explain this.

32:48.760 --> 32:49.600
I love this analogy.

32:49.780 --> 32:50.620
It's so good.

32:51.000 --> 32:52.780
Imagine you are in phase-eye.

32:52.780 --> 32:57.580
You are a ball sitting comfortably at the bottom of the deep, steep-sided bowl.

32:58.340 --> 33:03.120
If someone nudges you, say, a minor misunderstanding with a client, you roll up the side a bit,

33:03.200 --> 33:05.120
but gravity pulls you right back to the center.

33:05.520 --> 33:08.040
It's a self-correcting, highly stable basin.

33:08.260 --> 33:08.660
Right.

33:08.840 --> 33:13.280
But as your visibility increases, the shape of the bowl actually changes.

33:13.480 --> 33:15.360
It gets shallower and it starts to tilt.

33:15.620 --> 33:18.540
The energy landscape of your reputation changes fundamentally.

33:18.800 --> 33:19.080
Right.

33:19.080 --> 33:24.780
And in phase three, the bowl is basically flap and tilted severely downwards.

33:25.600 --> 33:33.680
A tiny nudge, a single poorly phrased tweet, sends the ball rolling completely off a cliff.

33:33.680 --> 33:38.180
And once you roll off that cliff, you can't just wish yourself back into the bowl.

33:38.700 --> 33:38.800
Yeah.

33:38.920 --> 33:44.300
The energy required to lift that ball back up the cliff and into a stable state is massive.

33:44.300 --> 33:50.640
What's particularly useful about Fliction's model is that it identifies an empirical signature

33:50.640 --> 33:54.640
right before this catastrophic phase transition happens.

33:54.760 --> 33:55.220
Oh, right.

33:55.360 --> 33:56.380
The warning sign.

33:56.660 --> 33:56.940
Yes.

33:57.520 --> 34:02.380
Just before a system goes unstable and tips from phase two into phase three,

34:02.580 --> 34:05.020
you see something called variance inflation.

34:05.540 --> 34:06.700
Variance inflation.

34:06.820 --> 34:11.540
I want to spend a minute on this because once I read this, I started seeing it everywhere online.

34:11.540 --> 34:15.440
What is the physical mechanism of variance inflation?

34:16.040 --> 34:20.940
Well, imagine a creator who's made the exact same kind of content for a year.

34:21.980 --> 34:26.440
Let's say it's a mundane video of them pouring coffee and talking about books.

34:26.580 --> 34:26.820
Okay.

34:27.060 --> 34:27.820
Super simple.

34:28.100 --> 34:28.320
Right.

34:28.720 --> 34:31.580
The input, the signal, hasn't changed at all.

34:31.780 --> 34:37.560
But suddenly, as visibility increases, you notice the comment section fracturing.

34:38.040 --> 34:41.120
People are arguing wildly about what the video means.

34:41.120 --> 34:48.440
Like one group accuses the creator of promoting a toxic hustle culture because they're up early making coffee.

34:48.580 --> 34:49.120
Exactly.

34:49.840 --> 34:55.180
And another group projects bizarre political motives on the brand of coffee they're using.

34:56.000 --> 35:02.000
People are reading massive conspiratorial intent into tiny, irrelevant details.

35:02.260 --> 35:06.620
The signal is suffering what Fliction calls semantic drift.

35:06.620 --> 35:07.300
Yes.

35:08.040 --> 35:14.220
The piece of work is identical, but the variance in how it is interpreted inflates dramatically.

35:14.720 --> 35:17.100
That is the exact feeling of semantic heating.

35:17.500 --> 35:22.160
You open a comment section on a viral TikTok where someone is literally just painting a chair,

35:22.460 --> 35:27.180
and there are 10,000 people fighting about late-stage capitalism and zoning laws.

35:27.300 --> 35:29.760
Meanwhile, the creator just sitting there holding a paintbrush.

35:29.760 --> 35:30.440
Exactly.

35:31.100 --> 35:34.300
That variance inflation is the warning siren.

35:34.480 --> 35:37.420
It's the water bubbling right before it turns to steam.

35:37.620 --> 35:38.280
Spot on.

35:38.580 --> 35:41.740
So if human bandwidth is the absolute bottleneck here,

35:42.000 --> 35:46.960
if we physically do not have enough hours in the day to correct this algorithmic distortion,

35:47.540 --> 35:50.580
the natural desperate next question is,

35:50.580 --> 35:56.060
can we outsource the correction to something that doesn't need to sleep?

35:56.060 --> 35:57.160
You mean AI.

35:57.640 --> 35:57.800
Right.

35:58.400 --> 36:02.080
Can artificial general intelligence fix the math?

36:02.300 --> 36:05.960
It is the ultimate sci-fi solution to a mathematical problem.

36:06.940 --> 36:11.280
If bounded human bandwidth is the fatal constraint in the no-go theorem,

36:11.700 --> 36:19.540
can a decentralized network of AGI's safely achieve global visibility without triggering reputational destruction?

36:19.540 --> 36:24.680
The paper specifically looks at Ben Gertzel's vision of an AGI ecology.

36:25.320 --> 36:28.860
He talks about this utopian idea of distributed resonance.

36:29.080 --> 36:29.300
Yes.

36:29.540 --> 36:34.860
The theory of distributed resonance suggests that if you have network of superintelligent AGI minds,

36:35.020 --> 36:39.020
they could process, verify, and align interpretations instantly.

36:39.180 --> 36:43.300
So they wouldn't suffer from human cognitive bottlenecks or emotional heuristics?

36:43.640 --> 36:44.680
Theoretically, yes.

36:45.240 --> 36:49.360
They could modify the thermodynamics of interpretation itself.

36:49.540 --> 36:54.380
Bypassing the turbulent phase 3 and entering a hypothetical phase 4.

36:54.520 --> 36:55.080
Phase 4.

36:55.420 --> 36:57.220
The resonant distributed regime.

36:57.900 --> 37:02.880
High visibility, massive scale, but magically zero entropy.

37:03.340 --> 37:05.560
Perfect understanding across the globe.

37:05.680 --> 37:07.140
But for that to work,

37:07.540 --> 37:12.220
the AGI would have to distribute its corrective capacity so broadly and perfectly

37:12.220 --> 37:16.320
that the rumor-to-truth asymmetry completely vanishes.

37:16.820 --> 37:21.360
Truth would have to travel just as fast and penetrate just as deeply as a rumor.

37:21.620 --> 37:22.120
Exactly.

37:22.120 --> 37:28.660
But Flixian completely drops the hammer on this idea with the re-emergence theorem for distributed systems.

37:29.100 --> 37:31.760
Even our AI saviors can't beat the physics.

37:31.760 --> 37:38.740
The re-emergence theorem states that even if you build highly advanced AGI's that are perfectly stable internally,

37:39.080 --> 37:43.060
meaning their own localized confucational domains have zero entropy,

37:43.440 --> 37:47.600
the absolute second that two different AGI domains touch,

37:48.100 --> 37:50.500
massive entropy is generated at the boundary.

37:50.500 --> 37:54.240
Let me try an analogy here to visualize this boundary problem.

37:54.480 --> 37:55.040
Go for it.

37:55.120 --> 37:59.740
Imagine you have two perfectly organized immaculate libraries.

38:00.780 --> 38:04.120
One library is organized using the Dewey Decimal System.

38:04.700 --> 38:09.900
The other library is organized using the Library of Congress Classification System.

38:09.960 --> 38:11.380
Okay, two different systems.

38:11.760 --> 38:12.060
Right.

38:12.420 --> 38:15.960
Internally, within their own walls, they are flawless.

38:16.860 --> 38:18.200
Zero entropy.

38:18.200 --> 38:20.960
Every book is exactly where it should be.

38:21.540 --> 38:23.380
But the moment you try to merge them,

38:24.080 --> 38:28.720
the moment a book from Library A needs to be filed in Library B,

38:28.980 --> 38:31.540
or the librarians try to share a catalog,

38:31.820 --> 38:33.680
you get total chaos at the borders.

38:33.800 --> 38:35.580
The interpretive frameworks collide.

38:35.840 --> 38:37.640
That is exactly the mechanism.

38:38.020 --> 38:42.840
Because different AGI systems will inevitably have different training data,

38:43.040 --> 38:47.080
different representational structures, or different optimization parameters.

38:47.080 --> 38:50.800
And when those frameworks interact, interpretive mismatch occurs.

38:51.080 --> 38:55.180
And wherever there is mismatch in a complex system, there is entropy.

38:55.680 --> 39:00.900
So AGI isn't a magic bullet that just suddenly makes the internet safe for virality.

39:01.160 --> 39:01.500
No.

39:02.060 --> 39:04.840
AGI isn't just about amplifying intelligence.

39:05.300 --> 39:06.480
As the paper puts it,

39:06.780 --> 39:12.260
trying to achieve safe virality through AI is an attempt at field engineering.

